# Infisical machine identity for this machine.
#
# Install as ~/.config/dotfiles/infisical-identity, mode 600, NEVER committed:
#
#   install -m 600 -D ~/.local/share/dotfiles/infisical-identity.example \
#     ~/.config/dotfiles/infisical-identity
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT local.env
#
# `infisical login` stores a user token in the OS keyring, and the keyring starts
# locked. That put the wallet on the LOGIN path: dotfiles-secrets.service needed an
# ordering dependency on a KDE-only unit, had to be deferred to
# graphical-session.target, and still failed intermittently — leaving every shell
# in the session with no secrets. A machine identity is exchanged for a
# short-lived access token over the network, so no local credential store is
# consulted and there is nothing to unlock.
#
# It is not local.env because local.env is built from required-env, and
# gen-local-env.sh emits an EMPTY PLACEHOLDER for every variable in that manifest.
# An empty client secret would fail every infisical call on a freshly provisioned
# machine — the same trap already documented for ATUIN_*. Secrets do not belong in
# that manifest at all.
#
# PROVISIONING (Infisical UI, once per machine)
#
#   1. Organization -> Access Control -> Identities -> Create identity
#      Name it after the machine, so it can be revoked without touching any other.
#   2. Auth method: Universal Auth. Create a client secret; the value is shown
#      ONCE. Set a TTL if the machine is not permanent.
#   3. Project -> Access Control -> add the identity, scoped to THIS project and
#      the `prod` environment only, read-only.
#   4. Paste the two values below.
#
# Verify with `dotfiles-secrets --check` — it prints which auth path is live, and
# reports `machine identity (no keyring)` when this file has taken effect. If it
# still says `CLI vault`, this file was not found or one half is missing.
#
# ROTATION: replace the client secret here and restart the fetch:
#   systemctl --user restart dotfiles-secrets.service && secrets-load
#
# HONEST LIMIT: the client secret is on disk, readable by this user. Against a
# same-user attacker that is no better than the plaintext it replaces. What it buys
# is blast radius — this credential is scoped to one project and one environment,
# is revocable for this machine alone from the Infisical UI, and yields only a
# short-lived token. A leaked user token is the whole account.

INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
